================================================================================================================
  PART 2 -- LONG/SHORT EXPOSURE AUDIT (read from each strategy's own real trade/position data)
================================================================================================================

  ORB gold RETEST: 554 trades, LONG 282 (50.9%), SHORT 272 (49.1%) -- takes BOTH sides, directional breakout, not structurally biased long by construction (whichever side of the opening range breaks first).

  On-chain BTC H=20: LONG-ONLY by construction (verified in run_onchain_signal.py -- entries trigger only on z > ADDR_Z_THRESHOLD, no short branch exists in the trade-building loop; section 30's own docstring states this a priori). 100% long when in a position, 0% short, flat the rest of the time (no_pos gate).

  Capped vol spread (Credit-spread FILTERED 1pct): 362 trades, PUT-CREDIT-SPREAD (bull-put, bullish-tilted short-vol) 181 (50.0%), CALL-CREDIT-SPREAD (bear-call, bearish-tilted short-vol) 181 (50.0%) -- BOTH books run concurrently with shared capital, approximating a short-strangle/short-vol structure. This is NOT a simple long or short directional bet -- it profits from range-bound markets and is hurt by large moves in EITHER direction, though the two legs' deltas are not guaranteed to net to exactly zero on any given day.

  Gold-silver pairs (N1.5): dollar-neutral pairs trade -- when in a position it is ALWAYS simultaneously long one leg and short the other (0.5x each, gross exposure 1x). Time in long-GLD/short-SLV: 26.7%, short-GLD/long-SLV: 36.9%, flat: 36.4%. Structurally market-neutral by construction, not a net directional bet on either metal or the broader market.

  COMBINED-PORTFOLIO EXPOSURE SUMMARY: of the 4 candidates, only ORB gold RETEST carries outright net directional risk on any given trade (but splits both ways over time, 51%/49% long/short). On-chain BTC H=20 is the ONE candidate that is structurally a pure long-only bet on BTC rising -- it has ZERO short exposure and is flat (not short) the rest of the time, so it offers NO protection if BTC falls; it can only avoid losing by being out of the market, not by profiting from a decline. The credit-spread and pairs strategies are both structurally close to market-neutral by construction (short-vol and dollar-neutral respectively), so the combined 4-strategy book is NOT simply 'long the market' -- but it also has no strategy in it designed to profit meaningfully FROM a bear market, only ones designed to avoid or be indifferent to one.

